{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling","id":936969,"title":"Grappling","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-25T00:27:54-07:00","id":936969,"title":"Grappling","content":"Hi all, <br /> I have a fast question about grappling move.<br /> Assuming that one of the 2 contenders wins the contested roll for the grapple control with 2 more successes then the opponent (in the contested roll strenght+brawl, the first one rolls 4 successes the second one rolls 6) and he take a damage move, the damage done is written be as the successes rolled by the winner.<br /> My doubt is: should the damage be just the successes in excess (2)? or is it all the successes obtained (6)? Because I think that the opponent effort, even if not enough to win the grapple, should be taken in account and mitigate the freedom to act of the opponent. But the book states &quot;successes rolled&quot; in a general way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 07-25-2016, 04:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T00:59:49-07:00","id":936977,"title":"","content":"In Contested Actions, the winner keeps all their successes unless the specific text says otherwise. Grapple doesn't.<br /> <br /> So RAW, the one that rolled 6 successes get 6 successes (on two maneuvers no less since it's an exceptional success).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T01:19:20-07:00","id":936979,"title":"","content":"Ok, thank you <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T07:46:50-07:00","id":937086,"title":"","content":"Heavy Arms is correct.<br /> <br /> However, grappling is one of the few recurring rolls that doesn't have a penalty built into it, circumventing the usual (Attribute + Skill + Other Stuff) - (Defense) that attacks and many skill rolls have. Rolls like this are hyper-affected by effects like rote actions. In addition, one of grappling's weaknesses is supposed to be that you have to &quot;waste&quot; a turn grappling the other guy, but Vampire and Werewolf both have auto-grapple via bite features when the combat gets heavy. All the while it locks both you and your opponent down, being excellent crowd control/damage mitigation in smaller fights. You can even damage the opponent twice, effectively circumventing the &quot;no multiple attacks&quot; rule by either doing the Damage move twice (disallowed under some interpretations) or by doing Damage once and then using Break Free to attack.<br /> <br /> For this reason, at least for Vampire and Werewolf games where grappling is so prominent, I'd recommend the following house rule: only net successes count for both the &quot;damage&quot; move and Dramatic Successes. Furthermore, the &quot;damage&quot; move cannot be selected twice in a dramatic success, although you can still use Damage then Break Free and use your free instant action to attempt an attack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11106-captain-shoe","name":"Captain Shoe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T08:22:12-07:00","id":937099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Captain Shoe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling?p=937086#post937086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, grappling is one of the few recurring rolls that doesn't have a penalty built into it, circumventing the usual (Attribute + Skill + Other Stuff) - (Defense) that attacks and many skill rolls have. Rolls like this are hyper-affected by effects like rote actions. In addition, one of grappling's weaknesses is supposed to be that you have to &quot;waste&quot; a turn grappling the other guy, but Vampire and Werewolf both have auto-grapple via bite features when the combat gets heavy. All the while it locks both you and your opponent down, being excellent crowd control/damage mitigation in smaller fights. You can even damage the opponent twice, effectively circumventing the &quot;no multiple attacks&quot; rule by either doing the Damage move twice (disallowed under some interpretations) or by doing Damage once and then using Break Free to attack.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not entirely accurate.<br /> <br /> Establishing a grapple still requires beating Defense. It just doesn't require you to continually do so because you can't dodge something that's already grappling you; Defense represents not getting hit after all.<br /> <br /> Vampires don't get a &quot;auto-grapple via bite,&quot; feature. They get an, &quot;auto bite on a successful grapple initiation,&quot; freature <i>while in frenzy</i>, which is a pretty limited option. Most vampires are not going to be focused on frenzy as a primary combat strategy.<br /> <br /> Werewolves only get the free grapple with an attack in 3 out of 5 forms. None of them are human-ish, which has limitation then and there, and one of them is Gauru form... which is supposed to be the killing form of doom (but has the whole timer issue).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T09:47:54-07:00","id":937151,"title":"","content":"Whether it's auto-grapple via bite, or auto-bite via grapple, it bypasses a main weakness of grappling: having to spend a round &quot;setting up&quot; the contested grapple. It's both crowd control and damage rolled into one package.<br /> <br /> In other words, a human ala Dwayne 'The Rock' Johnson would have to grapple his opponent, doing no damage on turn 1 (unless he got a dramatic success on the grapple attempt and then did Damage), but likely being content with just forcing the opponent to deal with him... either cos he's limiting the enemy side's damage output, or because he wants to tie his target up. Then, only in turn 2 can he begin to pile on some damage.<br /> <br /> Frenzying vampires and werewolves on the other hand, if lethality is their goal as it often is, get both the crowd control effect *and* some damage on Turn 1. Sucking some vitae too as a vampire, if I recall correctly. <br /> <br /> I'd recommend the house rule as compared to a mortals game, grappling will come up like 3x as many times in Vampire and 10x as many times in Werewolf. Mortals will usually be doing bashing with wrestling as well, while these supernaturals will be doing lethal. Of course if no vampire ever frenzies or bothers with riding the wave or if the werewolves spend the whole campaign in human forms (maybe because they have no Iron Masters to hide their nature from technology), then grappling shouldn't be an issue. However, for most campaigns I'd say you have a very high chance of one of the players making a bestial vampire or a pit bullish Jaws of Death werewolf as they are classic archetypes. After all, there is even an entire Ordo Dracul coil designed for controlling one's frenzy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11106-captain-shoe","name":"Captain Shoe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T16:58:01-07:00","id":937375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Captain Shoe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling?p=937151#post937151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whether it's auto-grapple via bite, or auto-bite via grapple, it bypasses a main weakness of grappling: having to spend a round &quot;setting up&quot; the contested grapple. It's both crowd control and damage rolled into one package.</div> </div> </div> </div>This presumes that the number of actions needed is meant to be a &quot;weakness&quot; as opposed to a baseline.<br /> <br /> Merits can override this as well. So a mortal tricked out for grappling has some options to do this as well (if not as good). Mortals have to work harder for it, but this isn't actually a purely supernatural thing.<br /> <br /> Ultimately... so what if supernaturals are better at something than mortals? They're supposed to be. It should be whether or not the given thing they're good at is thematically appropriate.<br /> <br /> Are vampires in frenzy being better at grabbing people to bite them thematically appropriate? Yes.<br /> <br /> Are werewolves in non-human forms being cable of what many real world predators can do (bite and hold on) thematically appropriate? Again, yes.<br /> <br /> There's no need to worry about &quot;balancing&quot; this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Frenzying vampires and werewolves on the other hand, if lethality is their goal as it often is, get both the crowd control effect *and* some damage on Turn 1. Sucking some vitae too as a vampire, if I recall correctly. </div> </div> </div>Yeah.. dangerous things are dangerous. Also vampires have to pick between drinking and damaging (which is going to be predicated on the kind of frenzy).<br /> <br /> And frenzied vampires/werewolves have tons of highly dangerous options at their disposal. The idea that grapples are particularly of note here really doesn't hold up.<br /> <br /> Finally, a frenzied vampire might theoretically gain the &quot;crowd control,&quot; benefit but they're not acting tactically in a frenzy. They're acting according to the desire of the Beast.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd recommend the house rule as compared to a mortals game, grappling will come up like 3x as many times in Vampire and 10x as many times in Werewolf. Mortals will usually be doing bashing with wrestling as well, while these supernaturals will be doing lethal. Of course if no vampire ever frenzies or bothers with riding the wave or if the werewolves spend the whole campaign in human forms (maybe because they have no Iron Masters to hide their nature from technology), then grappling shouldn't be an issue. However, for most campaigns I'd say you have a very high chance of one of the players making a bestial vampire or a pit bullish Jaws of Death werewolf as they are classic archetypes. After all, there is even an entire Ordo Dracul coil designed for controlling one's frenzy. </div> </div> </div>This paints a fairly large false dichotomy of, &quot;tons and tons of grappling,&quot; or &quot;none.&quot; Which is weird, and also pretty much irrelevant to any need to house rule it. House ruling it implies it's broken in some way that needs addressing.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T20:22:13-07:00","id":937428,"title":"","content":"I have no issues with supernaturals being badass. In fact, I love it! But incidentally, you are correct: I'm of the opinion grappling is an easy option... Perhaps a bit overpowered? You clearly are not of that opinion, and there is no problem with that.<br /> <br /> But that actually only plays a minor role in my recommendation: I don't think something should be house-ruled <i>just </i>because it's overpowered. One minorly overpowered combat option doesn't really matter when the other guy can just bring Retainers, Allies, Dominate legions of gangers, be permanently invisible, etc. I think on this we agree. <br /> <br /> My support of house rules on grappling are more just to get them in line with the rest of the system and/or have them make some more sense. And/or to have more cinematic fights.<br /> <br /> OP mentioned <b>&quot;...Because I think that the opponent effort, even if not enough to win the grapple, should be taken in account and mitigate the freedom to act of the opponent.&quot;</b> This is a valid reason to be interested in not following RAW. It just... <i>makes sense</i> that efforts counteract each other. Consider 8 successes vs 7 successes. One guy barely won. Yet the rules say that even though the other guy only got one more success, you basically do a critical hit and can do enough damage to break his neck, and then can do another Grappling Move on top of that. One of which can be an another attack, even though by all accounts &quot;multi-attacks&quot; are supposed have been toned down in 2e... reserved for ranged Autofire or powerful supernatural merits/powers. Another way of looking at it is that Grappling is a Contested Action that doesn't behave as a proper Contested Action <i>should.</i><br /> <br /> To me, 8 successes vs 7 successes resulting in 8 damage and a dramatic success (which is likely even more damage) would be the same shenanigans as if 8 stealth successes vs 7 perception successes resulted in a automatic free Killing Blow for the winning party. Which could work out pretty cool in a stealth-oriented game, but isn't my cup of tea. The system is lethal enough already.<br /> <br /> As an aside, it also bugs me that both grappled parties retain their dodge. Most other systems apply a minor penalty to their Defense equivalent. This can also be resolved by house-ruling a &quot;Grappled/Grappling Tilt/Condition&quot; that applies a -2 Defense to both parties.<br /> <br /> Anyway, these are just my thoughts. They're not even my original rules, all of these ideas are from these very forums. May you both play as you wish and happy gaming!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11106-captain-shoe","name":"Captain Shoe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-25T23:06:10-07:00","id":937492,"title":"","content":"*Pokes head in* you can't choose the same move twice on an exceptional success in a grapple.<br /> <br /> Carry on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-26T01:18:35-07:00","id":937511,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Captain Shoe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling?p=937428#post937428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My support of house rules on grappling are more just to get them in line with the rest of the system and/or have them make some more sense. And/or to have more cinematic fights.</div> </div> </div> </div>But they are in line with the rest of the system, because Contested Actions aren't ablative in the vast majority of cases.<br /> <br /> As for the cinematic fight thing... well the CofD isn't really cinematic in the first place. But the problem with proposing house rules that make it harder to use overpower maneuvers is that they make grappling boringly slow and plodding.<br /> <br /> Speaking from experience with 1e and playing a grapple focused combatant, my character's role in combat very quickly became, &quot;hold the biggest heavy hitter to keep them locked up, while the small fry are taken care of, and then the rest of the group will finish off the guy I'm holding.&quot; It took so long to actually get anywhere in a grapple that it was uncinematic and unfun.<br /> <br /> That's my issue with trying to house rule grappling to be less effective. You're doubling the rolling, for the sole purpose of making grappling even slower, which just means it drags on and on while other combat options are moving far more quickly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Consider 8 successes vs 7 successes. One guy barely won. Yet the rules say that even though the other guy only got one more success, you basically do a critical hit and can do enough damage to break his neck, and then can do another Grappling Move on top of that. </div> </div> </div>Two characters are having a jumping contest to see who can jump the highest. One rolls 8 successes and the other 7 successes. They both make extremely impressive high jumps, but the 8 success jump was still higher. They don't cancel each other out with the 8 success jump being treated as only scoring 1 success, and the 7 success jump not actually getting off the ground.<br /> <br /> The issue with it being a matter of, &quot;sense,&quot; is that the &quot;sense&quot; comes from treating the Contested roll as a purely mechanical thing with no in character aspect.<br /> <br /> 8s vs. 7s is, &quot;Two master wrestlers engage in a complicated chess match of quick shifts of center of gravity, holds, counter holds, and positions, until one finally secures a checkmate and delivers a devastating joint lock that crashes his opponent head first into the ground&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To me, 8 successes vs 7 successes resulting in 8 damage and a dramatic success (which is likely even more damage) would be the same shenanigans as if 8 stealth successes vs 7 perception successes resulted in a automatic free Killing Blow for the winning party. </div> </div> </div>That's not exactly a fair comparison. And 8 successes on Stealth vs. 7 successes to spot you still results in you sneak attacking with an exceptional success' bonus. So the sneak attacker still gets to deny the target their Defense, and would get whatever bonus the ST decides is appropriate for the Exceptional success (probably a positive Condition). The difference here, really, is just that there isn't a default bonus for exceptional successes on your sneak attack roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As an aside, it also bugs me that both grappled parties retain their dodge. Most other systems apply a minor penalty to their Defense equivalent. This can also be resolved by house-ruling a &quot;Grappled/Grappling Tilt/Condition&quot; that applies a -2 Defense to both parties. </div> </div> </div>Eh. To me it's a pointless wash for others trying to get involved in close-quarters. Any penalty the target would suffer from being in a grapple would also mean a penalty for the attacker to not hit the wrong guy as the grapple rolls around. If you're trying to hold someone down so they're easier for your friends to hit, there's default options that already do that. Go Prone and Hold, and they lose Defense while attackers get +2 dice on top of it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-26T05:58:15-07:00","id":937561,"title":"","content":"In terms of fidelity, when one person gets the upper hand in a grapple, like *really* gets the upper hand, the fight tends to be over. Not garnishing successes contributes to that while also accounting for someones ability to take advantage of the situation.<br /> <br /> In terms of game balance... what option are you comparing it to? Weapons deal more damage at lower ends and at higher ends can steak vampires in one hit. Firearms ignore defense and give you a psuedo aoe attack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-26T12:14:55-07:00","id":937720,"title":"","content":"Just tweak your definition of 'ablative' contested actions. Subtract successes when the stakes are zero sum. <br /> <br /> A standard marathon doesn't fit zero sum. Your success doesn't take away from the success of someone else. You have your time, they have theirs, whoever has the better time wins.<br /> <br /> Chess fits. There should feasibly be a dramatic (story-mechanical) difference between an (8) Successes vs (7) Successes game, and an (8) Successes vs. (1) Success game. The relative value of pieces lost per side, the swiftness of victory, etc. <br /> <br /> I put grapple in the zero sum game for sure just like normal combat. Sorry, but professionally trained fighters are harder to joint-lock/tap-out on a regular basis than white belt civilians. <br /> <br /> On Exceptional Grapples, I'm not a fan of the 'Do Damage' then break free and 'Attack' the same target though it reads allowed by RAW. Personally, I was happy Requiem shifted away from a lot of multiple-actions-per-turn mechanics and I don't like this holdout. It's probably white-room thinking, but I dislike exceptional frenzy grapplers being able to:<ul><li>Turn 1: Initiate Grapple + Lethal Damage + Vitae</li> <li>Turn 2: Damage or Feed + Break Free + Initiate Grapple + Lethal Damage + Vitae</li> <li>Turn 3: Repeat</li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-26T13:28:40-07:00","id":937744,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Holy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling?p=937720#post937720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just tweak your definition of 'ablative' contested actions. Subtract successes when the stakes are zero sum.</div> </div> </div> </div>At least for grappling, the problem is that most overpower maneuvers are binary success/failures. Winning by 1 success or 10 successes doesn't matter to, really, anything but Damage and special moves (Choke, Feed, etc.). So making it ablative's only really impact on most options is to reduce the odds of exceptional successes; which just gets back to my earlier issue with these sorts of house rules making grappling go painfully slow compared to the rest of the combat system.<br /> <br /> As well, &quot;zero sum,&quot; doesn't really apply. Grappling is only a zero sum situation in the control roll, because it is binary: only one side can gain control of the grapple. The overpower maneuvers are not inherently zero sum actions. In fact most of them are explicitly not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Chess fits. There should feasibly be a dramatic (story-mechanical) difference between an (8) Successes vs (7) Successes game, and an (8) Successes vs. (1) Success game. The relative value of pieces lost per side, the swiftness of victory, etc. </div> </div> </div>Why should there by a mechanical difference instead of a narrative difference? Rolling 8 successes deserves the bonuses of an exceptional success (such as plopping a negative Condition on your opponent to rattle them on the next match) because it's an insanely good game. Turning it into a 1 success result denies it that status. All the differences you need between winning by 1 or winning by 7 can be handled by description (since things like value of pieces, and speed of victory, are not mechanically important; with a caveat that if it is an Extended Contested roll off then speed of victory is already baked in by hitting your target number sooner).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sorry, but professionally trained fighters are harder to joint-lock/tap-out on a regular basis than white belt civilians. </div> </div> </div>Which is true already. Professionally trained fighters are going to have higher Defense to overcome to initiate a grapple, higher rolls to control the grapple, and possible Fighting Merits to boost their grapple skills.<br /> <br /> The game just avoids turning it into a nibble slog-fest of 1-2 successes on each turn to represent that struggle, but lets each turn's action do a lot more so grapples move faster (and really, on average, have the same overall effect).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On Exceptional Grapples, I'm not a fan of the 'Do Damage' then break free and 'Attack' the same target though it reads allowed by RAW. </div> </div> </div>RAW actually doesn't really as much this way as people seem to think. Succeeding on a Break Free just turns the Break Free action reflexive, it doesn't say it turns both actions reflexive.<br /> <br /> So, you can damage (instant action for your turn), and break free (reflexively for you and your opponent) and then you're done and they can act as they want. Boom, solved, still well within the RAW.<br /> <br /> That said:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Personally, I was happy Requiem shifted away from a lot of multiple-actions-per-turn mechanics and I don't like this holdout. It's probably white-room thinking, but I dislike exceptional frenzy grapplers being able to:<ul><li>Turn 1: Initiate Grapple + Lethal Damage + Vitae</li> <li>Turn 2: Damage or Feed + Break Free + Initiate Grapple + Lethal Damage + Vitae</li> <li>Turn 3: Repeat</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>This skips the fact that this is actually a mechanically stupid tactic. If you're good enough to reliably get exceptional successes on grapples (which lets face it, this conversation is a bit off track on the idea that a character is getting one every turn since that implies an exceedingly high investment in this that any equal investment in any other action would result in similarly dominating confrontations), you don't want to let go and give them a chance to Dodge with a WP to avoid your attempt to grab again, when you clearly are better off in the Strength + Brawl roll off that denies them other options.<br /> <br /> Every time you break free, you're giving your opponent a chance to break the cycle, when keeping them in the grapple ensures you can keep your advantage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-26T15:10:46-07:00","id":937791,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling?p=937744#post937744\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">At least for grappling, the problem is that most overpower maneuvers are binary success/failures. Winning by 1 success or 10 successes doesn't matter to, really, anything but Damage and special moves (Choke, Feed, etc.). So making it ablative's only really impact on most options is to reduce the odds of exceptional successes; which just gets back to my earlier issue with these sorts of house rules making grappling go painfully slow compared to the rest of the combat system.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">I wouldn't be so quick to move past those choke / feed / damage examples. Those right there already show me that success thresholds matter far more in grappling than many other contested rolls.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As well, &quot;zero sum,&quot; doesn't really apply. Grappling is only a zero sum situation in the control roll, because it is binary: only one side can gain control of the grapple. The overpower maneuvers are not inherently zero sum actions. In fact most of them are explicitly not. </div> </div> </div></span></span></span><br /> Some maneuvers have static and non-threshold effects, yes. I don't see how that speaks to my idea of zero-sum contests though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Why should there by a mechanical difference instead of a narrative difference? Rolling 8 successes deserves the bonuses of an exceptional success (such as plopping a negative Condition on your opponent to rattle them on the next match) because it's an insanely good game. Turning it into a 1 success result denies it that status. All the differences you need between winning by 1 or winning by 7 can be handled by description (since things like value of pieces, and speed of victory, are not mechanically important; with a caveat that if it is an Extended Contested roll off then speed of victory is already baked in by hitting your target number sooner).</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div>Not every heavyweight bout ends in a KO or injury. Not every master chess tournament ends in a Fool's Mate. Like, an Olympic tug-of-war match with an 8v7 game should be a mega-battle that barely drags the loser across the line. An 8v1 match should be slingshotting the loser like a broken resistance band across it. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">I think a skill-user should have fewer exceptional successes when facing tougher opponents / stronger efforts - even if they are always winning. Making them subtractive contested rolls does that. Tying it only to the win/loss condition doesn't.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Additionally, they have added dramatic and strategic moments to my games when players - even when seeing some big 8 successes grapple or discipline roll come their way - decide if they want to spend willpower. Not because they think they can beat the opponent's roll - but because they think they can at least downgrade the success level from Exceptional to Normal.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">I like that a lot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Which is true already. Professionally trained fighters are going to have higher Defense to overcome to initiate a grapple, higher rolls to control the grapple, and possible Fighting Merits to boost their grapple skills. </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The game just avoids turning it into a nibble slog-fest of 1-2 successes on each turn to represent that struggle, but lets each turn's action do a lot more so grapples move faster (and really, on average, have the same overall effect).</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div>See above.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">RAW actually doesn't really as much this way as people seem to think. Succeeding on a Break Free just turns the Break Free action reflexive, it doesn't say it turns both actions reflexive.</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">So, you can damage (instant action for your turn), and break free (reflexively for you and your opponent) and then you're done and they can act as they want. Boom, solved, still well within the RAW.</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div>Ah, this is a good point!</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, verdana, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Thanks for the discussion. In the end, I'm happy with my interpretation, even if it's more house-rule than RAW.</span></span></span></span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-26T18:51:05-07:00","id":937863,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Holy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling?p=937791#post937791\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wouldn't be so quick to move past those choke / feed / damage examples. Those right there already show me that success thresholds matter far more in grappling than many other contested rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>Choke is easy to move past because tt's a Merit only option that can be used multiple turns in a row to accumulate the successes. Forcing ablative grappling just makes choking people out slower if they're outclassing their opponents; it also makes Choke worth buying without having to be in exceptional 9+ dice pool ranges before you'd bother with it.<br /> <br /> Damage (including Feeding) are where number of successes matters. But that doesn't demonstrate a need to make the control roll ablative alone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Some maneuvers have static and non-threshold effects, yes. I don't see how that speaks to my idea of zero-sum contests though. </div> </div> </div>It's a matter of a misapplication of what zero sum means. Zero sum situations mean that any gain by one side results in an equal lose for the other side in a closed system. Tug of war is a zero sum game, because you can't pull rope towards you without pulling an equal amount of rope away from the other team. Chess is not a zero sum game (though there are zero sum strategies for it) because the whole point of the game is to strategically force trades that are uneven. If you force a trade where you take a knight for the loss of a pawn, the gain/loss on each side is not equal.<br /> <br /> CofD combat isn't a zero sum game. Gains are not inherently equal to the opponent's loses.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not every heavyweight bout ends in a KO or injury. Not every master chess tournament ends in a Fool's Mate. Like, an Olympic tug-of-war match with an 8v7 game should be a mega-battle that barely drags the loser across the line. An 8v1 match should be slingshotting the loser like a broken resistance band across it. </div> </div> </div>Not every bout/match ends in a massive success in the system because not ever roll generates an exceptional success. As I noted earlier, this tangent has taken an odd turn where there's an assumption that we're dealing with characters that are generating exceptional successes <i>all the time</i> in their given area. Even at 15 dice (normal rolls) the odds of exceptional success is ~2/3, not every roll.<br /> <br /> Again, 8 successes vs. 7 successes doesn't need mechanical differences from 8 successes vs. 1 success. Descriptive differences are plenty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think a skill-user should have fewer exceptional successes when facing tougher opponents / stronger efforts - even if they are always winning. Making them subtractive contested rolls does that. Tying it only to the win/loss condition doesn't. </div> </div> </div>You get fewer exceptional successes against tougher opponents already in many ways (some of which I already mentioned). If you're facing an equal, even if you're both at such high dice-pools you're regularly rolling 5+ successes, you'll get less exceptional successes than against someone with half your dice pool because you'll lose some of the roll offs.<br /> <br /> This is already in the system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Additionally, they have added dramatic and strategic moments to my games when players - even when seeing some big 8 successes grapple or discipline roll come their way - decide if they want to spend willpower. </div> </div> </div>This... is sort of a house rule. WP is supposed to be declared before you know the results, not after the fact, and the character should be aware of the need to push themselves.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-27T04:47:04-07:00","id":938024,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/936969-grappling?p=937863#post937863\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This... is sort of a house rule. WP is supposed to be declared before you know the results, not after the fact, and the character should be aware of the need to push themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div>As long as the PC hasn't rolled yet, I let them add to pools freely even if the environment/NPC rolled first. But no - player agency over when to spend the willpower is just as valid a move-choice. The character doesn't always have to 'understand' the mechanics of extra effort if the player knows/believes the moment is important.<br /> <br /> Re: zero-sum - okay, I see. Yeah, we're talking past each other. I don't mean perfectly exact cross-purpose of efforts - but for the overall success. There is no difference to me between two fighters engaging with swords vs engaging with fists vs. engaging in a grapple. Combat is a zero sum - your efforts subtract from mine in the chasing of victory. Only if you eliminate my capability in some fashion so I have no chance to effect your success does it stop being that zero-sum. Firearms at range is a perfect example. From my real life experience, grapple follows the same skill v skill process that swords/fists does. It's not a skill v target application like Firearms.<br /> <br /> In non-Combat applications it comes down to the question of 'When should an opponent's success threshold matter even when they lose the contest?' You go for pretty much never, seems. That doesn't clock with me. Thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}